          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "56\t\\paragraph{Cross-seed stability.} Balanced accuracy is stable across seeds (57--59\\%), but internal recall varies from 51.5\\% to 81.8\\%---the signal exists but is insufficient for practical routing, consistent with the death avoidance decomposition: when ${\\sim}90\\%$ of routing value is death avoidance, the learnable non-death signal is structurally thin.\n57\t\n58\t\\paragraph{End-to-end evaluation.} Deployed as an episode-level router (Table~\\ref{tab:baselines}), the trained router scores $-10.70$ (90\\% CI $[-15.25, -6.38]$) with 17.6\\% death rate, allocating 97.0\\% of episodes to deterministic computation. It did not significantly outperform always-deterministic ($-9.47$, $[-13.66, -5.11]$)---CIs overlap substantially. The few internally-routed episodes include cases where the agent dies, and misrouting cost exceeds the benefit of correctly routing rare internal-preferred episodes. This is a direct corollary of death avoidance dominance: any router that occasionally routes internally risks catastrophic failure, while always-deterministic avoids this risk entirely.\n59\t\n60\tThree observations follow. First, logit-level success rules out uninformative labels, pointing to the episode-to-step granularity mismatch as the classification head bottleneck. Second, in death-dominated environments, always-deterministic is near-optimal and any learned router must overcome asymmetric misrouting cost (death vs.\\ marginal score improvement). Third, when DAF $\\approx 90\\%$, router headroom is structurally limited to the ${\\sim}10\\%$ non-death signal."
            }
          ],
