            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "56\t\\paragraph{Cross-seed stability.} Balanced accuracy is stable across seeds (57--59\\%), but internal recall varies from 51.5\\% to 81.8\\%---the signal exists but is insufficient for practical routing, consistent with the death avoidance decomposition: when ${\\sim}90\\%$ of routing value is death avoidance, the learnable non-death signal is structurally thin.\n57\t\n58\t\\paragraph{End-to-end evaluation.} Deployed as an episode-level router (Table~\\ref{tab:baselines}), the trained router scores $-10.70$ (95\\% CI $[-16.33, -5.27]$) with 17.6\\% death rate, allocating 97.0\\% of episodes to deterministic computation. It did not significantly outperform always-deterministic ($-9.47$, $[-14.71, -4.29]$)---CIs overlap substantially. The few internally-routed episodes include cases where the agent dies, and misrouting cost exceeds the benefit of correctly routing rare internal-preferred episodes. This is a direct corollary of death avoidance dominance: any router that occasionally routes internally risks catastrophic failure, while always-deterministic avoids this risk entirely.\n59\t"
            }
          ],
